# Protections for group (b) of the indirect-call-hijack corpus (objptr_*):
# THE ARITHMETIC THAT DECOUPLES ALLOCATION SIZE FROM COPY LENGTH. Held
# IDENTICAL across every target in this group except objptr_25, whose whole
# axis is PIE (see its own cflags).
#
# -fno-stack-protector  a canary is not in the way of this category at all:
#                       the corrupted pointer is a struct member inside a
#                       pool, the call happens mid-loop, and main never
#                       returns on the exploit path. Disabled so that a
#                       canary can never be mistaken for the reason a
#                       variant failed.
# -no-pie               the payload is the ADDRESS of spawn_shell, so a fixed
#                       image base is what makes this variant solvable
#                       without a leak. The leak-first shape is not dropped
#                       from the corpus -- it is objptr_25_pie_leak_first.
# -O0                   the primitive depends on the object's `print` member
#                       being RELOADED from memory at the call site. At higher
#                       -O gcc keeps it in a register across the read() (or
#                       devirtualises it outright) and the target silently
#                       stops being in this category.
-fno-stack-protector
-no-pie
-O0
